body:
`material!` keywords, the struct, its constructor, the allowed-keyword list and the log
lines used to be five things you kept in sync by hand. Now they are one list of
declarations, and `@params` works exactly like `@storage`. One form to define, one to reuse.

```julia
@params MyMaterial struct MyPointParameters
    @inherit StandardParameters                      # δ, rho, E, nu, G, K, λ, μ, bc, dmg_params
    @log "yield stress" sigma_y = Inf                # keyword with default, goes to the log
    @derived C::SArray{NTuple{4,3},FT,4,81} = get_hooke_matrix(nu, λ, μ)   # computed, not a keyword, follows the float type
end

@params MyOtherMaterial MyPointParameters   # same parameters, another material
```
`material!(body; horizon, rho, E, nu, Gc, sigma_y=250.0)` then just works, typos are
rejected with a `"did you mean"`, and the fracture keywords come from the damage model
(`@dmg_params`), not from the material.

Breaking: the old `@params` link-only API, `StandardPointParameters` (every material has
its own …PointParameters now), the Float64-pinned parameters.

Major enhancements on how storages are initializes and used. The amount of boilerplate code is reduced.
Fixes #131, closes #129, closes #72, closes #70.

